ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ankylosing Spondylitis Non-radiographic - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute disc herniation with radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of radiating leg pain, normal straight leg raise, and intact lower limb reflexes.

Cauda equina syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact bowel/bladder control and normal perianal sensation.

Mechanical lower back strain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Chronicity of symptoms, prominent stiffness at rest rather than with movement, and concomitant enthesitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient reported acute, severe axial immobility and distress, raising red flags for potential neurological compromise. Urgent in-home physical examination was imperative to definitively rule out cauda equina syndrome and acute radiculopathy, which would require immediate surgical referral. Furthermore, the severity of the pain crisis necessitated prescription-level anti-inflammatory administration that could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period, satisfying urgent medical necessity.

